Potentiometric heads in the sub-Robinson Unit are below those in the Robinson coal and are also below the base of the coal. The upper part of the sub-Robinson may be dry or partially saturated in the vicinity of discharge areas. In these areas, the ground water is moving laterally to discharge at springs at the base of the coal, and vertical recharge to the unit has been short-circuited due to the comparatively high permeability of the overlying Robinson coal. Throughout most of the area there is hydrologic continuity between the sub-Robinson and the other aquifers.
The sub-Robinson units are recharged vertically from the overlying coal and, in Tract III, from lateral flow from the southeast. Discharge is to springs, the alluvium, and to deeper aquifers.
Underlying the upper sub-Robinson unit in the northern part of Tract II is an 80 to 250 foot thick dense shale. Throughout the rest of the project area this interval is composed of interlayered sandstone, siltstone, and shale. These shales are referred to as the lower sub-Robinson Unit (Westmoreland Resources, 1975 1, p. 41), or the Fort Union undifferentiated. Most of the domestic and stock wells in the area are completed in this unit at depths of 50 to 300 feet (Westmoreland Resources, 1975 1, p. 43). They generally yield less than 10 gpm (U.S. Bureau of Indian Affairs 1974, p.87). In August of 1975 a well yielding
